cutils-maytera.patch - MayteraOS target adaptation for QuickJS-ng cutils.h.

WHY. cutils.h selects its allocator header by platform. Because this port
cross-compiles as x86_64-linux-gnu, the __linux__ branch would #include
<malloc.h> (for malloc_usable_size), a header the MayteraOS freestanding libc
does not provide. This patch adds a MAYTERAOS branch (selected by -DMAYTERAOS in
the recipe's cflags) that is tested BEFORE the __linux__ branch and:
  - skips <malloc.h>, and
  - makes js__malloc_usable_size() return 0, exactly as cutils.h's own generic
    #else fallback does. QuickJS already handles a 0 here: it declines the
    in-place string-concat optimisation (quickjs.c uses the value as a >= guard)
    and reports 0 in JS_ComputeMemoryUsage stats. No behaviour is lost beyond
    those two, both of which are optional.

This is a target adaptation, not a bug fix. The engine source is otherwise
byte-for-byte unaltered, so there is no frozen copy of upstream to rot at the
next version bump: only these two small hunks.

--- a/cutils.h
+++ b/cutils.h
@@ -52,6 +52,8 @@
 #endif
 #if defined(__APPLE__)
 #include <malloc/malloc.h>
+#elif defined(MAYTERAOS)
+/* MayteraOS: no malloc.h; see ports/quickjs patch */
 #elif defined(__linux__) || defined(__ANDROID__) || defined(__CYGWIN__) || defined(__GLIBC__)
 #include <malloc.h>
 #elif defined(__FreeBSD__)
@@ -604,6 +606,8 @@
     return malloc_size(ptr);
 #elif defined(_WIN32)
     return _msize((void *)ptr);
+#elif defined(MAYTERAOS)
+    return 0; /* MayteraOS libc does not expose usable size */
 #elif defined(__linux__) || defined(__ANDROID__) || defined(__CYGWIN__) || defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__GLIBC__)
     return malloc_usable_size((void *)ptr);
 #else
